==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*yewg-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|disjoynen}}, from {{der|en|fro|desjoindre||disjoin}}, from {{der|en|la|disiungō}}, from {{m|la|dis-}} + {{m|la|iungō||join}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪsˈd͡ʒɔɪnt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-disjoint.wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔɪnt|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Not smooth or [[continuous]]; [[disjointed]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1894|author=Henry Gough|author2=James Parker|title=A Glossary of Terms Used in Heraldry|page=109
|text=Azure, a chevron '''disjoint''' or broken in the head or - BROKMALE. Per fesse gules and sable , a chevron rompu counterchanged - ALLEN, Sheriff of London}}
# {{lb|en|set theory|not comparable}} Of two or more [[set]]s, having no members in common; having an [[intersection]] equal to the [[empty set]].

